## 5. Results and Analysis

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 3 -->
### 5.1 Quantitative Results

Table 2 presents the quantitative results on all three datasets. Our EMFN model achieves superior performance across all metrics.

| Method | ImageNet-2023 | COCO-Multimodal | MIT-Indoor67 |
|--------|---------------|------------------|--------------|
| ResNet-50 | 87.2% | 82.5% | 78.9% |
| ViT-Base | 89.1% | 84.7% | 81.2% |
| CLIP | 91.3% | 87.2% | 83.8% |
| MMBT | 92.8% | 89.1% | 85.4% |
| **EMFN (Ours)** | **94.7%** | **91.8%** | **87.9%** |

Table 2: Performance Comparison on Benchmark Datasets {#tbl:results}

The results demonstrate that EMFN consistently outperforms baseline methods by 2-4% across all datasets, validating the effectiveness of our attention-based fusion mechanism.

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 3 -->
### 5.2 Ablation Study

We conduct an ablation study to analyze the contribution of different components:

$$
\text{Improvement} = \frac{\text{Accuracy}_{\text{full}} - \text{Accuracy}_{\text{ablated}}}{\text{Accuracy}_{\text{ablated}}} \times 100\%
$$

The attention mechanism contributes 2.3% improvement, while multimodal fusion provides 1.8% gain over unimodal baselines.

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 3 -->
### 5.3 Computational Efficiency

Figure 2 shows the trade-off between accuracy and computational cost measured in FLOPs (Floating Point Operations).

![Accuracy vs. Computational Cost](figures/efficiency_plot.png){#fig:efficiency width=70%}

EMFN achieves the best accuracy-efficiency trade-off, requiring 30% fewer FLOPs compared to MMBT while maintaining superior accuracy.

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 2 -->
## 6. Discussion

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 3 -->
### 6.1 Key Contributions

Our work makes several key contributions to multimodal deep learning:

1. **Novel Architecture**: The EMFN combines CNN and transformer strengths effectively
2. **Attention Mechanism**: Cross-modal attention enables better feature fusion
3. **Empirical Validation**: Comprehensive evaluation on multiple benchmarks
4. **Efficiency**: Reduced computational overhead compared to existing methods

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 3 -->
### 6.2 Limitations and Future Work

While EMFN shows promising results, several limitations remain:

- **Scalability**: Performance on datasets with more than 4 modalities is unclear
- **Interpretability**: The attention weights require further analysis
- **Domain Transfer**: Generalization to other domains needs investigation

Future work will focus on extending EMFN to handle temporal sequences and developing more interpretable attention mechanisms.

<!-- SECTION_LEVEL: 2 -->
## 7. Conclusion

This paper presents the Enhanced Multimodal Fusion Network (EMFN), a novel architecture for multimodal data fusion in computer vision. Through extensive experiments on benchmark datasets, we demonstrate that EMFN achieves state-of-the-art performance while maintaining computational efficiency. The attention-based fusion mechanism proves effective for combining complementary information from multiple modalities.

The success of EMFN opens new avenues for research in multimodal learning and suggests that hybrid architectures combining CNNs and transformers represent a promising direction for future computer vision systems.

